  "notes": "The solo run only, and it spans two production regimes the research treats as one model: the Bomb Squad-built 1990 record and the 1992–94 West Coast funk pivot. What survives both is the drum method, documented as a three-stage hierarchy — bottom beats assembled on an 8-track, then \"seasoned\" with additional percussion, then scratches — so this is the only entry in the batch with six perc lanes at 3–5 hits a bar and snare ghosts on all four 16th offbeats. The 1990–91 records swing harder and sit faster, which is why `bpm.max` is 102 against the lane's 100 and `swing` is 0.6. ⚠ The harmony is authored across both regimes on purpose: the 1990–91 material is riff/loop-modal with no functional progression and the 1992–94 pivot brings major-mode soul samples (Isley Brothers, The Moments) on diatonic 4-bar loops, so both a minor loop family and a major `I–vi–IV` family are present with `2_bars_per_chord` weighted top for \"fewer, longer loops\". ⚠ Sir Jinx's documented brief — keep the record from sounding East Coast — is turned into three numbers rather than a mood: melody register dropped to 67–89 for low-mid warmth, `articulation` legato for more sustain, and a chord voicing register capped at 73. `timbreHint` is `clean_guitar` for the live guitar and flute overdubs. ⚠ The bass is looped sampled bass plus added sub with live bass also used, so it is `independent_riff` but at a high `followRootsProb` of 0.6 — a sampled bass follows the record it came from. The hard cold opens are a 2–4 bar intro and `switchUpProb` 0.12 for the skit architecture. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 45 to 44 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 45 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
